    55	| `dao.stream.ringbuffer` | `src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc` | `dao.stream.ringbuffer` |
    56	| `dao.stream.ws` | `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc` | `dao.stream.ws` |
    57	| `dao.stream.rpc.client` | `src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc/client.cljc` | `dao.stream.rpc.client` |
    58	| `dao.stream.rpc.server` | `src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc/server.cljc` | `dao.stream.rpc.server` |
    59	| `dao.stream.rpc.ws` | `src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc/ws.cljc` | `dao.stream.rpc.ws` |
    60	| `yin.repl` | `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc` | `yin.repl` |
    61	
    62	The first three are the stream plan's deliverables and are listed only so the
    63	dependency is visible. The layout follows the repo's existing convention, where
    64	`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc` already coexists with `src/cljc/dao/stream/` — a
    65	namespace and a directory of the same name are not in conflict.
    66	
    67	New aliases in `deps.edn`, beside the existing ones and not replacing them:
    68	`:clj-yin-repl`, `:cljs-yin-repl`, `:cljd-yin-repl` and
    69	`:cljd-yin-repl-build`, mirroring the existing four with `yin.repl` as the
    70	main namespace and its own `:output-dir`/`:output-to` under `build/`. The cljd
    71	launcher `yin.repl.runner` — which shells out to the build alias and then runs
    72	the compiled Dart — gets a v2 counterpart, `yin.repl.runner`, at
    73	`src/clj/yin/repl/runner.clj`, pointing at the v2 build alias and its own
    74	`bin/` entry point.
    75	
    76	**On duplication.** `yin.repl` cannot share code with `yin.repl`, because
    77	sharing would mean editing `yin.repl` to extract it. Roughly 60% of the 1085
    78	lines — command parsing, value formatting, VM construction and reset, the
    79	compile and eval paths, `bracket-balance` — touch no stream and are copied
    80	verbatim. That is the price of leaving v1 untouched; it is bounded, and the copy
    81	dies when v1 does. Worth doing on the way in: put the copied stream-independent
    82	half in `yin.repl.core` and keep `yin.repl` to the parts that touch
    83	streams, so the new side is at least well factored even though the old side
    84	cannot be.
    85	
    86	## What the v2 RPC layer must be, and why it is not a port
    87	
    88	`dao.stream.rpc.*` cannot be moved to v2 by translation. Its central mechanism
    89	is unavailable:
    90	
    91	- **v1 reads its responses from the stream it wrote to.**
    92	  `rpc-client/wait-for-response` polls `next` on the same duplex
    93	  `WebSocketStream` that `call!` appended to. A v2 ws handle is writer and
    94	  closable only; the response is not there. It is on the deposit medium the host
    95	  composition wired.
    96	- **v1's cursors are arithmetic.** `advance-cursor!` compares `(:position
    97	  next-cursor)` against `(:position current)` with `>`, seeded from `(atom
    98	  {:position 0})`. v2 cursors are opaque and transport-minted, expose no
    99	  position, and there is no `seek`.
   100	- **v1 returns a Promise on cljs and a Future on cljd.** No v2 operation returns
   101	  a host-native async value on any host.
   102	
   103	So the v2 layer is greenfield, and its shape follows from the contract.
   104	
   105	**A client is constructed from two handles, not one.** A writer handle for the
   106	request path, and a reader handle plus its cursor for the response medium. That
   107	asymmetry *is* the v2 shape: under a transport with no reader surface, the two
   108	directions are two streams, and pretending otherwise is what produced the hidden
   109	per-socket inbox the ws spec forbids.
   110	
   111	**The client is a step, not a call.** Following 4b's settled step-versus-loop
   112	decision, and for the same reason — a loop that hands itself to a scheduler is a
   113	relocated callback:
   114	
   115	- `request!` appends the request and returns the next client state, a
   116	  correlation id, and the append outcome. A `full` is retryable and **must not
   117	  mint a new id**; `closed`, `invalid-value` and `transport-error` are terminal
   118	  for that request.
   119	- `poll!` drains the response medium up to a budget through its own cursor,
   120	  matches responses to outstanding ids, and returns the next state plus whatever
   121	  completed. `blocked` returns to the caller; that is the yield.
   122	- The caller's own loop drives both. A blocking convenience is possible on clj
   123	  and belongs to the caller, not to this layer — putting it here is how the
   124	  three-way host divergence grew the first time.
   125	
   126	**It must be total over the outcome algebra**, as `forward-step` is. In
   127	particular a `gap` on the response medium means outstanding responses may have
   128	been evicted: those ids are reported lost, as an outcome distinct from a
   129	timeout, because the two call for different responses.
   130	
   131	**The server is a step too.** `serve-step` drains the request medium, dispatches
   132	the operation against a handler map — the operation keyword remains only ever a
   133	lookup key into a map built in trusted process code, exactly as today — and
   134	appends each response to that connection's writer handle. No callbacks, and no
   135	`:conns` atom inside the transport: connection bookkeeping is the composition's,
   136	held explicitly.
   137	
   138	`dao.stream.rpc.ws` is a thin convenience over the two — wiring a client from
   139	an attached handle plus a deposit medium, and a server from an endpoint's
   140	request medium. It is written last, in R3 and R4, and only to the extent the
   141	REPL uses it: whatever generality v1's `dao.stream.rpc.ws` has beyond that is
   142	not reproduced on speculation.
   143	
   144	**Not in the v2 layer:** `retry` and `dedup`. They exist for the lossy UDP
   145	transport, there is no v2 UDP transport, and adding them now would be designing
   146	for a consumer that does not exist. They stay on v1 with `dao.stream.rpc.udp`.
   147	
   148	## Two decisions, before any code
   149	
   150	**D2 — Where a response travels.**
   151	The ws spec is explicit that a client's `append!` lands in a request medium only
   152	the serving host reads, and that nothing a client sends reaches other
   153	attachments. It does not say where a *reply to one client* goes.
   154	
   155	- *D2a — on that connection's server-side handle.* The accepted-connection
   156	  handle is a writer; the server appends the response to it. No forwarder, no
   157	  per-conversation stream.
   158	- *D2b — a per-conversation served stream.* On accept, `create!` a fresh stream,
   159	  drive `forward-step` from it to the connection, and append responses to it.
   160	  This is the shape the ws spec gestures at under per-conversation state, and it
   161	  needs the stream plan's 4b.
   162	
   163	**Recommendation: D2a.** It is the minimum that works, it needs no forwarder,
   164	and a reply addressed to one caller is not stream content anyone else should
   165	observe. D2b buys an outbound history a reconnecting client could replay, which
   166	matters only once resumption is specified — and resumption is deferred.
   167	
   168	**D3 — What the descriptor names.**
   169	`(connect "daostream:ws://localhost:8080")` is a URL; v2 attaches to a
   170	descriptor. The URL stays as user-facing sugar and normalizes to
   171	`{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws, :ws/host …, :ws/port …, :ws/path …}` — but a
   172	descriptor names a *served stream*, and under D2a the REPL endpoint forwards
   173	nothing outbound. Settle what `/repl` names and what the handshake's
   174	authoritative disclaimer answers for an unserved path. This depends on the
   175	stream plan's descriptor-key-set gate and cannot be settled before it.
   176	
   177	## Prerequisites — only what the REPL uses
   178	
   179	This slice is deliberately narrow. From
   180	`dao.stream.implementation-plan.md` it needs four things and no more:
   181	
   182	- **Phase 1's protocols and result convention** — the five handle operations as
   183	  surface-scoped protocols, the two transport entry functions, outcome maps
   184	  under `:dao.stream/outcome` with the contract's closed sets. *Not* the
   185	  declaration-driven conformance harness: that is the stream plan's own
   186	  deliverable and proves nothing this REPL needs. When it exists, the v2 ring
   187	  buffer and ws transport should pass it; nothing here waits for it.
   188	- **Phase 2, the ring buffer** — `create!`, `cursor`, `next`, `append!`,
   189	  `close!`, both anchors, evict-oldest with declared capacity, and `gap` with a
   190	  recovery cursor. The REPL's local streams and every deposit medium are ring
   191	  buffers. `lag` is already deferred and is not wanted.
   192	- **Phase 4a, the ws transport, both ends** — the client handle, the
   193	  accepted-connection handle, the transport constructor, and the boundary
   194	  adapter. This is the whole point of the slice.
   195	- **The cljd v2 ws transport.** The stream plan targets clj and cljs and says
   196	  cljd "follows once the slice is proven," so nothing there produces it. This
   197	  plan needs it, so **this plan owns it**: `dao.stream.ws` gains its cljd
   198	  implementation here, over `dart:io`, to the same spec as the other two hosts.
   199	  It is scoped by what the REPL uses and nothing more, and it is the one place
   200	  this slice reaches back into the transport layer.
   201	- **Both decision gates.** The REPL is a real client and a real server over a
   202	  real socket; it cannot be written against an unsettled handshake, close code,
   203	  or codec, and `connect` cannot build a descriptor whose keys are provisional.
   204	
   205	**Not prerequisites**, and worth naming so nobody blocks on them:
   206	
   207	- **Phase 3** (the ring-buffer descriptor round trip through a host-kept
   208	  directory). The REPL attaches to a ws endpoint by URL, never to a ring buffer
   209	  by descriptor, and composes its media directly rather than resolving them.
   210	  The *descriptor-key-set gate* is needed; the phase that produces its evidence
   211	  is not, and the `:ws/…` half of that decision is the half the REPL exercises.
   212	- **4b** (`forward-step`) — not under D2a. Nothing in the REPL forwards a stream
   213	  to a socket.
   214	- **4c** (the serving composition) — the REPL's serving shape is a request
   215	  medium and an interpreter, with no served-stream forwarding, and R4 builds it.
   216	- **Phase 5** — this plan *is* the end-to-end proof, on code someone runs.
   217	
   218	Phases R1 and R2 need only Phase 2 and can be written while the gates are still
   219	open. R3 and R4 need 4a and the gates.
   220	
   221	## Phase R1 — `dao.stream.rpc`
   222	
   223	The layer, over two ring buffers, with no socket at all — which is what makes it
   224	transport-agnostic by construction rather than by claim.
   225	
   226	- `client.cljc`: `init-client` from a writer handle plus a reader handle and
   227	  minted cursor; `request!`; `poll!`; `close!`. No cursor arithmetic, no
   228	  `{:position n}` literal anywhere, no promise on any host.
   229	- `server.cljc`: `serve-step` over a request medium and a per-connection writer,
   230	  with a handler map supplied by the caller.
   231	- Total over both outcome algebras, including the `gap`-loses-pending rule.
   232	
   233	Deliverable: request/response between two ring buffers on clj, cljs and cljd,
   234	with one shape on all three — no promise, no future, no `Completer`.
   235	`dao.stream.rpc.ws` waits for R3 and R4, which is where its two ends actually
   236	get built.
   237	
   238	## Phase R2 — `yin.repl`, local only
   239	
   240	No socket, no wire, no RPC. The local shell, on v2 streams.
   241	
   242	- Local streams are `create!` against `dao.stream.ringbuffer` with a declared
   243	  retention nature, replacing `{:dao.stream/type :ringbuffer, :capacity nil}`.
   244	  Choose and record the capacity; "unbounded" is a declaration, not an omission.
   245	- The telemetry and output drain loops mint cursors with `cursor` and advance by
   246	  the successor `next` returns.
   247	- No `closed?`: `repl-state` reports what the last operation returned rather
   248	  than asking.
   249	- Every `append!` call site reads its outcome instead of discarding it —
   250	  including the output stream, where a `gap` is what a lagging reader is owed.
   251	
   252	Deliverable: a working local REPL — no `connect`, no `serve!` — on all three
   253	hosts, requiring no v1 namespace: `clj -M:clj-yin-repl`,
   254	`clj -M:cljs-yin-repl`, and `clj -M:cljd-yin-repl`.
   255	
   256	## Phase R3 — The client side
   257	
   258	- `connect` normalizes the URL to a descriptor (D3), calls `attach!`, and gets a
   259	  handle back at once. It no longer blocks on clj, and returns no promise on
   260	  cljs or cljd.
   261	- The composition wires the deposit medium before attaching, then builds the RPC
   262	  client from the ws handle (writer) and that medium (reader). Admission is
   263	  declared at assembly per the ws spec.
   264	- **`Connected to …` moves.** It is printed when the REPL observes `:ws/opened`
   265	  on the deposit medium, not when `connect` returns. `:ws/not-found` reports
   266	  that the endpoint disclaimed the stream and is not retried;
   267	  `:ws/transport-error` reports a reachability failure that may succeed on
   268	  retry. The distinction the user sees is the one the medium carries.
   269	- `disconnect` is `close!` on the handle. Reattaching with the same descriptor
   270	  is ordinary `attach!`, and the deposit medium — with its cursor — outlives the
   271	  socket.
   272	
   273	`(telemetry "ws://…")` — routing telemetry to a *remote* sink — is not in this
   274	phase. It is a second ws consumer that proves nothing `connect` has not already
   275	proved, and it is listed under *Deliberately out of this slice*. Local telemetry
   276	to a stream or to stderr works from R2.
   277	
   278	## Phase R4 — The server side
   279	
   280	The serving composition, owned by the REPL and not by the transport.
   281	
   282	- **The endpoint's resolution table** maps the served path to what the REPL
   283	  serves (D3). Host-owned data.
   284	- **The request medium**: each accepted connection's inbound messages are
   285	  deposited, enveloped with `:ws/attachment`, onto a stream the composition
   286	  wired. One medium per attachment isolates a flooding client, which for a REPL
   287	  server facing many sessions is the safer default.
   288	- **The eval interpreter** drives `serve-step` against the REPL's handler map
   289	  and appends each response to that connection's handle (D2a), correlated by
   290	  attachment identity.
   291	- **Serving lifecycle**: what starts and stops an endpoint and who owns it,
   292	  replacing the `:conns` atom and the `:on-connect`/`:on-disconnect` callbacks.
   293	  Stopping closes the connections the composition itself tracks; that state is
   294	  the composition's, held explicitly.
   295	- **How a connection's attachment identity reaches the interpreter** is an open
   296	  question in `dao.stream.ws.md`, not in this plan. R4 cannot be finished until
   297	  that spec says where a server-side handle's identity comes from.
   298	
   299	## Phase R5 — End to end, aliases, and the docs
   300	
   301	Two processes, which is what the REPL always was.
   302	
   303	Start a server with `clj -M:clj-yin-repl --port 8080 --headless`; connect from
   304	a second process with `(connect "daostream:ws://localhost:8080")`; evaluate;
   305	disconnect; reconnect. Then verify, as separable facts:
   306	
   307	1. Evaluation round-trips, and two concurrent clients get their own answers —
   308	   correlation by attachment identity is what makes that true.
   309	2. Killing the connection is observable on both sides: the client handle answers
   310	   `closed`, the server's boundary deposits the departure.
   311	3. Reattaching with the same descriptor reaches the same endpoint, and the
   312	   client's cursor **on its own deposit medium** resumes across the socket's
   313	   death.
   314	4. A server-side `close!` of what it serves is distinguishable from a dropped
   315	   connection — the client deposits `:ws/ended`, not `:ws/closed`.
   316	
   317	The four `deps.edn` aliases and the v2 cljd runner land here. A **new**
   318	`src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md` documents the v2 REPL; the existing
   319	`yin.repl.md` is left alone, because the v1 REPL it describes still works and
   320	still ships. The new document differs in one observable way that must be stated:
   321	`connect` returns immediately, and the connection's outcome is reported when it
   322	is known.
   323	
   324	## Host matrix
   325	
   326	**clj, cljs (Node), and cljd — all three, first class.** Every phase passes on
   327	every host before it is done. This is the one place the slice is not narrow, and
   328	deliberately so: the REPL is the surface people run on each host, and the v2
   329	contract's central claim is that it means the same thing everywhere.
   330	
   331	- **R1 and R2** are pure `.cljc` with no transport, so a parity break there is
   332	  cheapest to find there. They gate on all three.
   333	- **R3 through R5** need the ws transport on each host: JVM and Node from the
   334	  stream plan's 4a, and cljd from this plan (see *Prerequisites*). Node server
   335	  plus Node client is the cljs slice; browser-client behaviour is not validated
   336	  by a Node target and stays out.
   337	- **cljd is where v2 pays for itself.** v1's `rpc-ws/connect!` returns a client
   338	  on clj, a `js/Promise` on cljs, and a `dart:async Future` on cljd, and
   339	  `await-connected` reimplements polling three times with a `Completer` on the
   340	  Dart side. In v2 there is one shape on all three and none of that gets
   341	  written. A cljd host that behaves like the other two is the strongest evidence
   342	  this plan can produce.
   343	- **The `#?(:clj …)` trap applies throughout.** `#?(:clj …)` does *not* exclude
   344	  code from the cljd build — the host-eval pass sees `:clj` — so any branch that
   345	  must not reach Dart is written `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)` with `:cljd` first;
   346	  `:cljd` in tail position silently fails. `dao.jing.remote` and the parts of v1
   347	  that are `#?(:clj …)`-gated are worth checking against this before they are
   348	  taken as precedent.
   349	- Per host, name explicitly how transient send-buffer `full` is detected, how a
   350	  send racing a concurrent close is handled, and that the codec produces
   351	  structurally identical values on all three. The cljd answers come from
   352	  `dart:io`.
   353	- The Flutter REPL surface (`src/cljd/yin/repl/flutter.cljd`) is v1's and is not
   354	  touched. A v2 counterpart is a separate, later piece of work — the v2 REPL
   355	  runs on cljd through `:cljd-yin-repl` without it.
   356	
   357	## Boundary of this plan
   358	
   359	**Untouched, explicitly** — no edits, no deletions, no deprecation markers:
   360	
   361	- `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc` and everything under `src/cljc/dao/stream/`,
   362	  including `rpc/` and `ws.cljc`.
   363	- `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`, `src/clj/yin/repl/runner.clj`,
   364	  `src/cljd/yin/repl/flutter.cljd`.
   365	- Their tests, and the existing `deps.edn` aliases.
   366	- `dao.jing.remote`, which reaches `dao.stream.rpc.*` from
   367	  `dao.jing.coordinate` and thence from `dao.space.index`. It keeps working on
   368	  v1 and gets its own plan later. It is the harder migration: its `call!` is
   369	  synchronous-on-JVM by design and `jing/materialize!` and `jing/get` dispatch
   370	  through it as ordinary function calls that return values, so either those call
   371	  sites learn to poll or that adapter keeps a JVM-only blocking shim. The REPL
   372	  does not have that problem because it already owns a polling loop.
   373	
   374	Deliberately out of this slice — REPL features that exist in v1 and are not
   375	being rebuilt yet, because each is a separate consumer of the transport and none
   376	proves anything `connect` does not:
   377	
   378	- **`(telemetry "ws://…")` to a remote sink.** Local telemetry — to a stream or
   379	  to stderr — works from R2; routing it over a socket is a second ws client.
   380	- **`(compile …)` and `(reset)`** if they turn out to touch a stream path this
   381	  slice has not built. They are local and should come across with the copied
   382	  half; if either drags in something else, drop it and say so rather than
   383	  widening the slice.
   384	- **The Flutter REPL surface.** cljd itself is in scope; that one *widget* is
   385	  not, and the v2 REPL runs on cljd without it.
   386	
   387	Not in this plan, by design:
   388	
   389	- **`retry`, `dedup`, and a v2 UDP transport.** They belong together and none of
   390	  them has a consumer yet.
   391	- **WebSocket-level resumption.** Deferred in `dao.stream.ws.md`. The v2 REPL
   392	  rejoins and continues; it replays nothing.
   393	- **Flow control between REPL client and server.** Deferred with the stream
   394	  plan's 4d and its dependency chain.
   395	- **Authentication or admission for who may connect.** The contract reserves
   396	  nothing for it, so neither does this plan. `--host 127.0.0.1` remains the only
   397	  boundary, exactly as in v1.
   398	
   399	## End condition
   400	
   401	This plan is complete when, **on each of clj, cljs (Node) and cljd**, a v2 REPL
   402	server accepts a connection from a second process, evaluates forms sent to it,
   403	and survives a disconnect and reattach — with `yin.repl` requiring no v1
   404	namespace. R5's four facts are the test of it, run per host.
   405	
   406	A cross-host pair is the stronger check and worth running once the three agree:
   407	a cljd client against a JVM server, and a Node client against a cljd server. The
   408	descriptor crossed a codec and the wire is the same wire, so if that fails the
   409	contract has not been implemented — it has been implemented three times.
   410	
   411	Remote telemetry, the Flutter surface, forwarding, flow control, and every other
   412	consumer follow their own plans.
   413	
   414	**Coexistence is the expected end state of this plan, not a defect.** Both REPLs
   415	ship, both alias sets work, and `dao.stream.rpc.*` keeps serving
   416	`dao.jing.remote`. Deleting v1 is the stream plan's end condition and happens
   417	when its last consumer has migrated, each under its own plan — of which this is
   418	the first.

