     1	# DaoStream Implementation Plan — the WebSocket Slice
     2	
     3	Status: migration plan, derived from and subordinate to `dao.stream.md`
     4	(the contract, review-approved) and `dao.stream.ws.md` (the WebSocket
     5	specification). Where this plan and the contract disagree, the contract
     6	wins. This document is transient: it is consumed as the phases complete,
     7	while the contract remains. Revised against the `gpt-5.6-sol` plan review
     8	of 2026-08-30 (`collab/review-dao-stream-plan.gpt-5.6-sol.stdout.log`).
     9	
    10	## Strategy
    11	
    12	Two deliberate narrowings:
    13	
    14	1. **A `dao.stream` namespace, not an in-place rewrite.** The legacy
    15	   `dao.stream` has 52 `open!` call sites and every subsystem as a
    16	   consumer; rewriting it in place is an atomic big bang. The new
    17	   namespace is `dao.stream` — one new file,
    18	   `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc` — building the contract greenfield while
    19	   legacy keeps the system running.
    20	   Consumers migrate one at a time, later, under their own plans. The
    21	   legacy implementation and its tests are evidence about behavior, not
    22	   constraints: no compatibility facade, alias, or dual protocol.
    23	2. **The WebSocket slice, end to end, and nothing else.** One vertical
    24	   slice that forces the contract's riskiest promises early: the
    25	   descriptor round trip across a real serialization boundary, Model A
    26	   serving, writer-only surfaces, deposit admission, and observable
    27	   teardown. Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, RPC) wait until
    28	   this slice stands.
    29	
    30	The slice still requires a minimal reader-surface core — the served stream
    31	and the deposit destination are both ring buffers — so the ring buffer is
    32	in scope, but only what the contract requires of it, nothing the legacy
    33	one happens to have.
    34	
    35	## Settled before Phase 1
    36	
    37	Three questions a phase would otherwise be forced to invent under delivery
    38	pressure. Each is decided here so no implementer decides it alone.
    39	
    40	**`attach!` is synchronous and never waits.** The Result Convention makes
    41	every operation return an outcome map; the contract's IO Model generalizes
    42	it — no operation waits. No transport in this slice returns a promise, a
    43	future, or a host-native async value from any of the seven operations, on
    44	any host. A ws `attach!` answers what is locally decidable — `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`,
    45	and `transport-error` for a handler that fails here and now — while how the
    46	connection resolved arrives as deposited
    47	lifecycle data and through the handle's own `append!` outcomes; see
    48	`dao.stream.ws.md`'s Operations section, corrected to match. A promise
    49	leaking out of a v2 operation is a defect, not a host accommodation.
    50	
    51	The rule covers `create!` equally, though this slice never exercises it:
    52	the ring buffer is in-memory and creates synchronously on every host, and
    53	the ws transport has no `create!` at all — it owns no stream, and starting
    54	an endpoint is a host composition step. A later transport whose creation
    55	needs host resources it cannot acquire synchronously (binding a port,
    56	opening a handle through an async-only API) answers the same way `attach!`
    57	does: the logical stream's identity exists at once, the handle reports
    58	acquisition state through its own operations, and the result of the
    59	acquisition arrives as data. Nothing waits.
    60	
    61	**Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated.** The contract's
    62	public surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy, and must
    63	not gain one — a predicate answer about a live handle is exactly what
    64	`closed?` was retired for. So the boundary constructor takes the deposit
    65	destination *and its declared retention nature as data*, derived from the
    66	same creation specification that produced the handle, and refuses a
    67	reject-mode declaration at assembly. This is configuration provenance, not
    68	runtime introspection. Runtime behavior is unchanged: every non-`ok`
    69	deposit result tears the connection down.
    70	
    71	**Logical-stream identity is minted in Phase 2.** `cursor-mismatch` in
    72	Phase 2 and stable descriptor identity in Phase 3 are the same identity.
    73	It is allocated when a logical stream is created, and both cursors and
    74	descriptors denote it. Their *representations* need not match and need not
    75	be equally serializable — cursor serialization across hosts stays TBD per
    76	the contract — but they must refer to the same thing.
    77	
    78	## Phase 1 — Contract core (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`)
    79	
    80	The protocol and data surface, with no transport. The seven operations are
    81	two kinds, and the split is the point:
    82	
    83	- **Five handle operations**, as protocols a handle implements per its
    84	  declared surface: `descriptor` (universal identity), `cursor` and `next`
    85	  (reader), `append!` (writer), `close!` (closable).
    86	- **Two transport entry functions**, `create!` and `attach!`, which take no
    87	  handle because none exists yet. These are per-transport functions.
    88	  Dynamic selection is a host-owned map from `:dao.stream/type` to a
    89	  `{create attach}` pair — ordinary data, ordinary lookup, performed by the
    90	  host. DaoStream ships no dispatch machinery: no multimethod, no registry,
    91	  no load-time side effect. Host dispatch is documentation plus an example
    92	  map in tests.
    93	
    94	Putting `create!` or `attach!` on a protocol is how the retired registry
    95	grows back; the file must not do it.
    96	
    97	Also in Phase 1:
    98	
    99	- The result convention: every operation returns an outcome map keyed by
   100	  `:dao.stream/outcome`, open maps, all qualified keywords under
   101	  `:dao.stream/…`, per-operation closed outcome sets exactly as the
   102	  contract's tables state, with no outcome held open for a mechanism that does
   103	  not exist.
   104	- Generic envelope validation: `:dao.stream/type` present and qualified;
   105	  everything else transport-owned.
   106	- `descriptor` on every handle regardless of surface — identity is
   107	  universal and outlives an attachment, so it must not be bundled into the
   108	  reader or writer protocol.
   109	
   110	Deliverable: a contract-conformance test suite, **declaration-driven by
   111	construction**. Its input is a manifest — ordinary data, not code the harness
   112	inspects — carrying the transport's entry functions and handle factories, its
   113	declared surfaces, its per-operation outcome subsets with a reason for every
   114	exclusion, and a fixture able to induce each outcome it does declare.
   115	
   116	The harness runs the blocks the manifest licenses: result shapes, envelope
   117	validation, and descriptor identity always, since `descriptor` belongs to no
   118	surface; reader laws (cursor provenance — mint / successor / gap-recovery only
   119	— non-destructive multi-reader reads, anchors) only for a declared reader;
   120	writer laws only for a writer; **close laws only for a declared closable**,
   121	which the earlier "always" wrongly assumed of every handle.
   122	
   123	Two assertions come from the manifest rather than from any one transport: no
   124	observed outcome falls outside the declared subset, and for each declared
   125	outcome the fixture actually produces it. That is what makes the contract's
   126	"declare which outcomes you produce, and why you exclude the rest" checkable
   127	rather than aspirational — an exclusion whose substitute never materializes
   128	fails, and an outcome that never appears proves nothing on its own.
   129	
   130	The suite is *authored* in Phase 1 and first *demonstrated* in Phase 2.
   131	Phase 1 has no transport, so it proves nothing on its own; that is expected
   132	and is not a reason to defer writing it.
   133	
   134	## Phase 2 — Ring buffer reference (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc`)
   135	
   136	The reference reader+writer+closable transport, scoped to the contract:
   137	
   138	- Logical-stream identity minted at `create!`, per *Settled before Phase 1*.
   139	- Evict-oldest retention with declared capacity; `append!` never returns
   140	  `full`; monotonic retention; `gap` with earliest-retained-else-tail
   141	  recovery cursor. Eviction applies whatever any reader is doing — a
   142	  retention policy that waits for readers is forbidden, not deferred (see
   143	  *Boundary of this plan*).
   144	No high-water figure in the creation specification: the ring buffer holds no
   145	reader positions and so can never act on one. The threshold at which a reader
   146	considers itself behind is that reader's own configuration, belonging to
   147	whatever interpreter measures it — which this slice does not build.
   148	- Cursor anchors `:dao.stream/oldest` and `:dao.stream/newest`;
   149	  `invalid-anchor`; cursors carry the logical-stream identity
   150	  (`cursor-mismatch` / `invalid-cursor` detectable); valid across handles
   151	  of the same logical stream.
   152	- One coherent state per operation (single atom; one deref per `next`),
   153	  satisfying the Concurrency section.
   154	
   155	Explicitly absent: waiters, drain, take, seq views, `closed?`, **live
   156	resize** — compatible with the contract but proving nothing the slice needs,
   157	since fixed declared capacity is sufficient for deposit admission — and the
   158	transport-owned **`lag`** operation, whose only consumer was flow control (see
   159	*Not in this plan*). Both follow the slice.
   160	
   161	The Phase 1 conformance suite runs here for the first time against a transport
   162	declaring all three surfaces. Laws that need a resolvable descriptor wait for
   163	Phase 3's directory; everything else runs now. Ring-buffer-specific tests cover
   164	retention, anchors, and eviction reporting.
   165	
   166	## Phase 3 — Descriptor round trip
   167	
   168	The portable identity story, still in-memory, and constructible without
   169	WebSockets: the contract explicitly permits a host composition to keep a
   170	directory, which is what makes a ring buffer's descriptor resolvable on its
   171	own host.
   172	
   173	- `descriptor` on a ring buffer handle yields a plain-data envelope that
   174	  survives the transit codec structurally unchanged.
   175	- A **host-kept local directory** — a map from stream identity to live
   176	  handle, owned by the test composition, not by DaoStream and not by any
   177	  transport. It is deliberately test-only: the ws serving side in Phase 4
   178	  resolves `:ws/path` against its own served-stream table and does not
