   190	settle it in `dao.stream.md`, and settle `:ws/…` in `dao.stream.ws.md`
   191	against it. Provisional keys carried into a wire format under delivery
   192	pressure become permanent by accident.
   193	
   194	**Decision gate — wire contract.** The ws spec's Handshake section says the
   195	wire's exact form is not specified yet, and its Deferred list holds the
   196	pieces: how the served stream's identity is presented on connect, the exact
   197	form of the authoritative disclaimer, the application-range close code that
   198	distinguishes an ended stream from a dropped connection, the value codec, and
   199	what a receiver does with wire input that fails to decode. 4a builds both
   200	ends of that wire and must not start before they are settled — in
   201	`dao.stream.ws.md`, where they live — for the same reason as the descriptor
   202	gate: until then two independently written implementations cannot
   203	interoperate, no wire-level conformance test can be written, and provisional
   204	choices carried into a wire format under delivery pressure become permanent
   205	by accident. The gate settles what the Deferred list already scopes; it adds
   206	nothing to that list and invents no protocol here.
   207	
   208	## Phase 4 — WebSocket transport, forwarding, and serving composition
   209	
   210	Per `dao.stream.ws.md`, both ends. Three deliverables in three layers —
   211	transport, infrastructure, composition — deliberately separate:
   212	
   213	### 4a — The transport (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc`)
   214	
   215	- **Client side**: `attach!` connects and returns without waiting, answering
   216	  only what is decidable here and now — `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`, or
   217	  `transport-error` for a handler that fails locally; see *Settled before
   218	  Phase 1*. The handle is writer+closable only; `append!` with transient
   219	  `full` (including while the connection is still establishing),
   220	  `invalid-value` for unencodable values, `closed` once down; `close!`
   221	  means disconnect, reattachable;
   222	  connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited.
   223	- **Server side**: the accepted-connection handle — writer+closable, minted
   224	  from a socket the runtime handed over, so neither `create!` nor `attach!`
   225	  produces it — with the same `full`, `closed`, `transport-error`,
   226	  send-racing-close, and identity rules as the client handle; plus that
   227	  connection's own boundary adapter for its inbound events.
   228	- **Transport constructor**: the entry functions a host puts in its dispatch
   229	  table are closures this constructor produces, having received the deposit
   230	  destination and its admission declaration. Bare namespace vars could reach
   231	  that state only through namespace globals.
   232	- **Handshake**: encode and recognize the served-stream presentation and the
   233	  authoritative disclaimer as settled at the wire-contract gate and recorded
   234	  in `dao.stream.ws.md` — wire protocol, so
   235	  both ends of it are transport, not composition.
   236	- **Boundary adapter**: deposits every inbound event — payload and
   237	  lifecycle, including how the connection resolved, judging nothing — into the
   238	  host-composed deposit destination — one medium per boundary in this slice,
   239	  the granularity being composition policy per `dao.stream.ws.md` — as
   240	  `:ws/attachment`-tagged envelopes — the same value `attach!` returns under
   241	  `:dao.stream/attachment`, per `dao.stream.ws.md` — enforcing admission at assembly
   242	  against the declaration passed alongside the destination, and tearing the
   243	  connection down on any non-`ok` deposit result.
   244	- No callbacks in the public surface; the legacy `on-open!`/`on-message!`/
   245	  `on-close!` shape does not reappear.
   246	- **Dependency check**: `v2/ws.cljc` requires only `dao.stream` — never
   247	  the ringbuffer namespace. The ws transport depends on the protocols; the
   248	  ring buffer is the slice's composition partner (deposit destination,
   249	  served stream), wired by the host composition, not by the transport. A
   250	  transport requiring another transport is the legacy registry defect
   251	  reborn.
   252	
   253	### 4b — Forwarding (`src/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc`)
   254	
   255	Serving is what makes a stream remotely attachable, and nothing in the
   256	transport transfers a served stream's values to a connected socket. Under
   257	the contract that transfer is an interpreter: it reads one stream through
   258	its own cursor and appends to another. Streams notify no one, so nothing
   259	drives it from below — a driver does, by calling it.
   260	
   261	That transfer is not WebSocket-specific. It reads *any* reader handle and
   262	appends to *any* writer handle: ring buffer to socket, ring buffer to ring
   263	buffer, the code is identical. Its transport-agnosticism is the proof that
   264	it belongs neither inside a transport nor inside one composition. It is a
   265	third layer between them.
   266	
   267	The step-versus-loop question is settled: the deliverable is a **single
   268	step**, `forward-step`, not a self-rescheduling loop. A loop that hands
   269	itself to a scheduler is a relocated callback — a continuation handed to
   270	host machinery to invoke later — and it needs a cancellation mechanism
   271	nothing here names. A step needs neither: a driver calling a function
   272	repeatedly is ordinary control flow. `forward-step` consumes the source
   273	handle, the destination handle, its state — the cursor to read from — and
   274	the two policies below; it drains at most the batch budget and returns its
   275	next state: the cursor to continue from, plus a status saying either that it
   276	should be called again (progress made, or a retryable `blocked` or `full`)
   277	or that it is finished, with the terminal condition carried as data.
   278	
   279	`forward-step` owns the three disciplines that are the same everywhere and
   280	are the easiest things in this slice to get wrong:
   281	
   282	- `full` **does not advance the cursor** — nothing was appended, and
   283	  treating the value as forwarded drops it silently;
   284	- `blocked` returns to the driver rather than spinning — on a cooperative
   285	  event loop a spin starves the runtime, so the send buffer never drains
   286	  and `full` never clears; returning is the yield;
   287	- `end` returns the terminal state `source-ended` and closes nothing.
   288	  `forward-step` is transport-agnostic, so it cannot select the close code
   289	  `dao.stream.ws.md` requires for an ended served stream — a generic
   290	  `close!` is a plain disconnect, and the client's fast path would silently
   291	  degrade to reattach-and-be-disclaimed. The transport-specific close
   292	  belongs to the composition that reads the state and knows its transport
   293	  (4c).
   294	
   295	It owns nothing else. Two policies are arguments, because each has different
   296	right answers in different compositions:
   297	
   298	- **the `gap` policy** — resume at the recovery cursor, or terminate. A
   299	  serving pump may skip evicted values; an interpreter that dropped
   300	  submitted work must not. Same outcome, opposite correct responses;
   301	- **the batch budget** — how many values to drain before returning, which
   302	  exists only because a cooperative event loop needs yielding.
   303	
   304	Everything else the loop form would have owned belongs to the **driver**,
   305	the composition-supplied code that calls `forward-step`. Cadence is the
   306	driver's — "run this again later" is `setTimeout` on Node, a sleeping thread
   307	on the JVM, and the concern of the runtime driving the interpreter, never of
   308	the stream (contract, *The readiness extension*). Starting is the driver's
   309	first call; nothing self-installs. Stopping is the driver ceasing to call it
   310	while keeping the returned state — which is also the cancellation mechanism —
   311	and restarting is calling it again with that state. The kept state's cursor
   312	never moved, which is why a future flow-control layer needs no protocol for
   313	saying where to continue: the sender never lost its place, so a paused
   314	connection resumes without being told anything.
   315	
   316	`forward-step` is written against the contract rather than against a named
   317	transport, so it is the one interpreter that must be **total over the outcome
   318	algebra** — every outcome of `next` and of `append!`, including those its
   319	particular endpoints never produce. Beyond the disciplines above that means an
   320	explicit branch for destination `closed`, `invalid-value`, and
   321	`transport-error`, and for source `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor` (its
   322	start cursor is caller-supplied and may not belong to the source), and
   323	`transport-error`. These return a terminal state carrying the outcome rather
   324	than retrying: only `full` and `blocked` are the retryable answers. Otherwise
   325	the contract's totality is bought by moving partiality into its first
   326	consumer.
   327	
   328	Testable with two ring buffers and no socket, so it may be written any time
   329	after Phase 2; only its *use* is Phase 4.
   330	
   331	### 4c — The serving composition (not in `v2/ws.cljc`, not in `forward.cljc`)
   332	
   333	What is left once the step is infrastructure is the wiring and policy only
   334	the host has. This is a named deliverable, not test wiring, and it must
   335	define:
   336	
   337	- the serving lifecycle: what starts and stops an endpoint, and who owns it;
   338	- the `:ws/path` → served-stream resolution table, host-owned data;
   339	- which identities this endpoint serves — the resolution table's contents.
   340	  The handshake's wire vocabulary is not composition; it belongs to 4a;
   341	- how a connection is associated with the served stream it joined, and how
   342	  that identity reaches deposited events;
   343	- one forwarder per connection — a driver stepping `forward-step` from the
   344	  served stream to that connection's ws handle — with the cadence, batch
   345	  budget, and `gap` policy this composition chooses;
   346	- whether inbound payload events are interpreted into appends on the served
   347	  stream, and if so by which interpreter — the boundary itself judges
   348	  nothing;
   349	- close behavior: what a server-side owner closing the logical stream does
   350	  to connected attachments. When a connection's forwarder returns
   351	  `source-ended`, this composition performs the transport-specific close —
   352	  the ended-stream close code per `dao.stream.ws.md`, settled at the
   353	  wire-contract gate — which `forward-step` deliberately cannot (see 4b).
   354	
   355	## Phase 5 — The slice, end to end
   356	
   357	Two processes, not two compositions in one. A same-process socket test is
   358	worth writing earlier for diagnostics, but it cannot show that a descriptor
   359	is self-contained or that no accidental shared state carries identity
   360	across — and those are central contract promises.
   361	
   362	Process A creates a ring buffer, serves it over ws, appends. Process B
   363	receives the descriptor as data, attaches, and appends outbound; B's host
   364	boundary deposits inbound traffic into B's medium; an interpreter in B
   365	reads that medium with cursors. State the bootstrap channel explicitly:
   366	how B obtains A's descriptor is part of the test, not an assumption.
   367	
   368	Kill the connection, then verify four separable facts:
   369	
   370	1. Detachment is observable on both sides — B's handle answers `closed`,
   371	   A's boundary deposits the departure.
   372	2. The served stream survives on A, and `attach!` with the same descriptor
   373	   rejoins the same logical stream.
   374	3. Two simultaneous attachments to the same stream carry distinct
   375	   `:dao.stream/attachment` values in their `attach!` results, and every event
   376	   deposited for one — payload, resolution, and lifecycle alike — carries that
   377	   same value under `:ws/attachment`.
   378	4. B's cursor **on B's deposit medium** still resumes — that medium
   379	   outlives the socket. The cursor is not handed to `attach!` and not used
   380	   through the ws handle, which has no reader surface. WebSocket-level
   381	   resumption — what history a rejoining client receives — is deferred in
   382	   `dao.stream.ws.md` and is not tested here.
   383	
   384	## Host matrix
   385	
   386	- **Phases 1–3** are pure `.cljc` with no transport: they must compile and
   387	  pass on clj, cljs (Node), and cljd. Nothing in them is host-specific, and
   388	  a parity break here is cheapest to find here.
   389	- **Phase 4–5** target clj and cljs (Node) — Node server plus Node client is
   390	  the slice. Browser-client behavior is not validated by a Node target and
   391	  is out of the slice. cljd ws follows once the slice is proven; beware the
   392	  `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)` reader-conditional trap.
   393	- **`forward.cljc` is host-agnostic** — `forward-step` takes no scheduler
   394	  and has no host dependency at all; cadence lives in the driver — so it
   395	  compiles and its ring-buffer-to-ring-buffer tests pass on all three hosts,
   396	  including where the ws transport does not yet exist.
   397	- Per host, name explicitly: how transient send-buffer `full` is detected,
   398	  how a send racing a concurrent close is handled, and that the codec
   399	  produces structurally identical values across all three.
   400	
   401	## What v1 callers lose, and what replaces it
   402	
   403	The contract is a clean-room redesign: `dao.stream.md` neither mentions v1 nor
   404	owes it compatibility, and nothing in `dao.stream` may consult this table.
   405	It lives here because this plan is the migration, and because the 52 `open!`
   406	call sites need to know what happened to the call they were making. It is
   407	consumed with the rest of this document.
   408	
   409	| v1 (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`)     | v2                            | Why                                                                                       |
   410	|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
   411	| `IDaoStreamReader` — `next`         | reader — `cursor`, `next`     | Cursors are minted by the stream instead of built inline as `{:position 0}`, so a transport owns its cursor representation and `cursor-mismatch` becomes detectable. |
   412	| `IDaoStreamWriter` — `append!`      | writer — `append!`            | Unchanged in shape; the outcome set is now exhaustive and `append!` never throws on a closed stream — it answers `closed`. |
   413	| `IDaoStreamBound` — `close!`        | closable — `close!`           | Outcome set is `{ok}`, idempotent.                                                        |
   414	| `IDaoStreamBound` — `closed?`       | **gone**                      | A predicate answer is stale the moment it returns; operate the handle and read the outcome. |
   415	| `IDaoStreamWaitable` — 2 fns        | **gone**                      | No readiness extension. A `blocked` reader retries when it chooses; cadence belongs to the runtime driving it. |
   416	| `IDaoStreamDrainable` / `drain-one!` | **gone**                     | Destructive read makes one reader's progress every other reader's data loss.               |
   417	| `open!` multimethod, `defopen` macro | **gone**                     | Ambient namespace-global registry with load-time registration. Replaced by an ordinary host-owned map from `:dao.stream/type` to `{create attach}`, looked up by the host. |
   418	| `open!` on a descriptor              | `create!` or `attach!`        | v1 conflated them. Creation takes a creation specification; attachment takes a portable descriptor and never creates. |
   419	| `take!!`                             | **gone**                      | Blocking. JVM-only by construction, and unimplementable on cljs/cljd.                      |
   420	| `strict-vec`                         | **gone**                      | Throws on ordinary operational signals; those are outcomes.                                |
   421	| `->seq`                              | **gone**                      | An interpreter over a stream, not part of the contract (see the contract's Composition).   |
   422	| bare `:ok` / `:blocked` / `:end` / `:daostream/gap` returns | outcome maps under `:dao.stream/…` | Every operation returns an open map keyed by `:dao.stream/outcome`, so extensions add keys without breaking consumers. |
   423	| `{:position 0}` cursors              | transport-owned cursor values | There is no `seek`; every valid cursor comes from the stream.                              |
   424	| `descriptor` via handle metadata     | `descriptor` operation        | Identity is universal, answered by every handle, and outlives any attachment.              |
   425	
   426	Migrating a call site is therefore mostly mechanical — `open!` splits, results
   427	become maps, cursors come from `cursor` — with two places that need a decision
   428	rather than a translation: anything relying on `closed?` must be restructured
   429	to act on an outcome, and anything relying on `drain-one!` or `take!!` needs a
   430	cursor and a retry policy its runtime owns.
   431	
   432	## Boundary of this plan
   433	
   434	Carried, with a term:
   435	
   436	- The slice's boundary deposits into a ring buffer rather than into the
   437	  medium ADR 0003 names, under a time-boxed exception that ADR records. The
   438	  exception ends when a conforming writer answers with data and a composition
   439	  indexes and publishes what the boundary deposits. Until then this plan is
   440	  where the exception is being spent, so it is named here and not only there.
   441	
   442	Forbidden, not deferred:
   443	
   444	- **Retention that waits for readers.** A stream that declines to evict until
   445	  every attached cursor has passed a position is one line from the design
   446	  above and has a failure mode none of the rest do: a single stalled or
   447	  departed reader holds the stream open indefinitely, and a hostile one does
   448	  it on purpose. Bounded retention must stay bounded. Loss is reported as
   449	  `gap`; it is never prevented by waiting.
   450	
   451	Not in this plan, by design:
   452	
   453	- Migration of any consumer (dao.runtime, yin.vm, dao.jing, dao.space)
   454	  — each gets its own plan against the finished slice.
   455	- Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, apply/RPC layers).
   456	- The waiter/readiness extension, ws resumption protocol, cursor
   457	  serialization across hosts, live ring buffer resize — deferred in the
   458	  contract, deferred here.
   459	- Gating who may attach. The contract reserves nothing for it, so neither does
   460	  this plan; it would arrive as an addition, not as the filling-in of a slot
   461	  left open here.
   462	- **Flow control — a reader pausing a sender.** Deferred, with its dependency
   463	  named rather than left as a date. A pause has to be a lease and not a switch:
   464	  a switch has a stuck state, since a lost resume or a reader that dies
   465	  mid-pause leaves the sender stopped forever, while a grant that lapses absent
   466	  renewal returns the system to sending on its own. Those semantics now live in
   467	  `dao.lease.md`, whose facts — the grant, its renewals, and the release or
   468	  reclaim left as a trace — are datoms on a medium, which for this system means
   469	  `dao.space`. `dao.space` is not yet built against this contract, so the chain
   470	  bottoms out beyond this slice.
   471	
   472	  What the slice ships without it is the honest default the contract already
   473	  specifies: a reader that falls behind is evicted past and told so with a
   474	  `gap`. That is right for a live feed and wrong only as the *sole* option, so
   475	  its absence costs an alternative rather than a correctness property.
   476	
   477	  Two items travel with it. The **pause wire vocabulary** — which the ws spec's
   478	  Deferred list calls interoperable wire content needing a durable home — is
   479	  therefore not owed by the wire-contract gate, and the gate's enumeration
   480	  stands as written. And **liveness probing** stays deferred in
   481	  `dao.stream.ws.md` on that document's own terms; this plan sets no interval
   482	  and no unanswered-probe threshold, because a subordinate transient document
   483	  may not settle what its superior left open.
   484	
   485	  Resuming it needs three things in order: `dao.space` on the v2 contract, a
   486	  `dao.lease.md` that claims the pause and liveness semantics its *Neighbouring
   487	  deferrals* section currently declines, and a reconciliation of the pause
   488	  request with that contract's negotiation — a reader asking "hold for *n*" is
   489	  a proposal, and a proposal creates no state until the serving side grants.
   490	
   491	## End condition — v2 is not a permanent fork
   492	
   493	The v2 namespace exists to protect the running system during migration,
   494	not to live forever. The slice is complete when Phases 1–5 pass on clj and
   495	cljs — Phase 4 being 4a, 4b and 4c, flow control having been deferred out of
   496	it — and it is explicitly **incomplete on cljd** until the cljd ws transport
   497	lands, so the end condition is not met before then. Flow control is not part
   498	of the end condition and does not hold it open.
   499	
   500	When the slice is complete and the last consumer has migrated (under its
   501	own plan), legacy `dao.stream` is deleted and a single decision is taken
   502	explicitly: `dao.stream` is renamed to `dao.stream`, or keeps its name
   503	permanently. An undecided coexistence of both namespaces is a defect of the
   504	migration, not a steady state.

